                        <div>
                            <h3 id="mp-rideshare-db">Ride Share Database</h3>
                            <p class="mp-proof-sub">Where RideFlow started. An eight-table schema I designed from scratch, deployed with a PL/SQL script, then queried and automated in both Oracle and Snowflake.</p>
                            <ul class="mp-facts">
                                <li><b>8</b><span>TABLES</span></li>
                                <li><b>9</b><span>FOREIGN KEYS</span></li>
                                <li><b>15</b><span>CHECK RULES</span></li>
                                <li><b>9</b><span>INDEXES</span></li>
                            </ul>
                            <ul class="mp-proof-list">
                                <li>Business rules live in the schema, not the app: fares must equal initial minus discount, trips must end after they start, and each driver gets one bank account</li>
                                <li>Re-runnable PL/SQL deploy script, with seven sequences feeding primary keys as column defaults</li>
                                <li>Every foreign key indexed, plus two lookups I chose for the queries that run most: ride request time, and vehicle make and model</li>
                                <li>Analytics with ROLLUP subtotals, HAVING, correlated and inline subqueries, CASE tiers, and DENSE_RANK and ROW_NUMBER window functions</li>
                                <li>Snowflake Scripting: a stored procedure that commits or rolls back, cursors that return result sets, and a user-defined function for ride counts</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
